The sunrise gives the sea a blazing golden sheen, and all the city - from the far harbor shanties to the splendid square just below you - basks in its light. The breeze blows fresh and brisk off the water, the song of nightbirds gives way to the chatter of clerks and touts, and your black-veined hands, twisted like talons, tremble on the windowsill. 

Behind you, men bicker about tests and shins, about a rot that has reached the heart and brain and cannot be cut out.

