Indigo waves rise in the wake of the huge marteling whale bearing down on Sagus Cliffs. Its flank blocks out the sun; its shadow sweeps over your meager dhow and a whole swath of sea.

From high up on the beast's back, a lookout leans down. "Plague!" he cries, in a voice that seems too faint, even considering the distance. His warning is unnecessary: the yellowjack flag flapping from the mast can be seen all the way from the city.

As the lookout stares directly at your dhow, he cocks his head quizzically and rubs his eyes, plainly confused to see no one in the boat. The light-bender on your belt does its work well... while its charge lasts.

After a moment, the man wanders off.

{ART: The Whale.}
{[A] SOUND: Waves lapping against the dhow.}

You hustle down the lanes that wind their way up the whale's head, until you are almost flush upon the forcefield. You cannot quite see what lies beyond it: shifting rainbow hues hint at a humanoid shape, but that means little: clouds in the heavens can seem human, too.

The shield radiates enough heat to warn against touching, and the flashes engulf any detritus that brushes up against it. The only thing that touches it unharmed is a ribbon of power, running from the forcefield all the way to the distant sterncastle. No doubt that is where the shield is generated.

{[B] SOUND: Maybe an energy buzzing sound with occasional small "zapping" sounds, like a bug being fried by one fo those bug lights.}

At last you arrive at the sterncastle, a towering edifice of steel and stone. That it is a seat of power would be clear even without the ribbon of energy streaming from its tower toward the distant howdah.

A large mob has gathered on the sterncastle steps, and many of the men and women mutely pound upon the gates. Their silence, and the flatness of their affect, is striking. As you watch their ineffectual siege, more file in behind you, blindly forcing you forward, cutting off any means of retreat.

For now, thanks to the light-bender, they have not seen you. {[A] SOUND: Pounding of fists/clubs on gate.}

The blast blinds, deafens, destroys. When your senses return, nothing remains of the mute men and women but flesh and blood spattered on the ground. Their victim, too, is gone, having fled in the confusion. One of the hovels is now rubble.

The explosion opened a way straight to the sterncastle, where a far larger mob has gathered. They are unmoved by the violence you've wrought and silently pound upon the gates with crude tools or just their fists. More file in behind you, forcing you forward, foreclosing retreat.

{[A] SOUND: Crystal exploding.}

The chiurgeon nods and gestures you toward him. "Sit, please," he says, pointing to a simple wooden chair.

As you rest for a moment, he begins his work with cyphers that are far from simple; relics, perhaps, of some more prosperous time. The care you receive is quick but effective, and at least some of your wounds are gone.

{[B] SOUND: A healing cypher being used. Perhaps there is a generic sound from the game we can use here?}

Wordlessly, unresistingly, the boy lets you hurl him over the side of your boat. Surely he can swim, but he makes no effort, and soon sinks into the indigo sea.

You hoist the lanteen sail and it fills with wind. Moments later, the whale is already dwindling into the distance. You do not bother to watch it or the dying village on its back, for ahead of you grows the great city of Sagus Cliffs, its buildings, then its teeming people coming slowly into view.

{[A] SOUND: A splash of a person being thrown into water. No attempt to swim or anything; might as well be a corpse thrown in.}

You quickly explain what the Healer has given you, and hand the inoculation to the Whalesinger. He presses the needle through his skin, blending the serum with his blood.

There is no change or wonderment worked by the injection: no esotery lies behind this treatment, merely a child's life. All the same, the Whalesinger nods and thanks you. "You have safeguarded my song, and thus the Haven."

{[B] SOUND: High-tech auto-injector sound sound, maybe more of a hiss sound than anything, like the injectors on Babylon 5.}

As you slip loose your windslice blade, the Whalesinger pulls a sword off his own from a sheath on his back. It is a humble weapon and age-worn, but its wielder is strong and desperate to survive.

He is but a singer, and you are a battle-honed killer; battle-honed, but battle-weary, and beset by the many wounds you've taken today. That your guard might slip is forgivable, but no less fatal for it.

{[B] SOUND: Two swords being drawn and clanging together. If we don't get to this sound, we could use ths sword brawl sound here.}

As you slip loose your windslice blade, the Whalesinger pulls a sword off his own from a sheath on his back. It is a humble weapon and age-worn, but its wielder is strong and desperate to survive.

He puts up some fight, but in the end, the man is a singer, while you are a killer. You make it as quick and painless as you can. The whale takes no notice of his death, and merely glides on its new course, at last unbound and free to wander as it will.

{[B] SOUND: Two swords being drawn, and clanging together. See 108 note.}

Abruptly, the collar around your neck flickers and a voice warns, "You have taken too long. Far too long."

Barely are the words spoken when the cliffside cannons open fire. There is no pain, only a moment's realization and then oblivion. Neither you nor the whale will leave any trace to trouble the city.

{[A] SOUND: Cannons firing.  We're talking about scifi energy cannons, not age of sail cannons.}

As the cypher strikes the forcefield, there is a flash of light and a thunderclap - one that seems all the louder for the oppressive silence that preceded it. 

Your senses return, revealing that you have gotten no good from your effort. The forcefield shimmers on, unperturbed; the umbilicus of energy running from the sterncastle is uncut; and your crystal is now no more than drit blowing in the sea breeze.

{[A] SOUND: Crystal explosion.}

Snug around your neck, a steel collar trembles, and a voice whispers in your head: "Hurry. Our seaward batteries can turn the whale to mist if they must, but better to save our fire for a darker day."

After a moment, the collar buzzes again, and the voice adds, "Eliminating contagion is doctors' work, and no doctor would use a saw when a scalpel will do. Go, now, cut what you must, and make sure that this sickness does not reach our city." Finally, the voice says, "And don't worry: your body can't fall prey to their disease." {VOICE: I'd add a sneer on "contagion."}

The collar goes still. The whale's leathery side looms above you like a living cliff. Somewhere up there are the men and women carrying death toward your home.

At last you arrive at the sterncastle, a towering edifice of steel and stone. That it is a seat of power would be clear even without the ribbon of energy streaming from its tower toward the distant howdah.

A large mob has gathered on the sterncastle steps, and many of the men and women mutely pound upon the gates. Their silence, and the flatness of their affect, is striking. As you watch their ineffectual siege, more file in behind you, blindly forcing you forward, cutting off any means of retreat.

The light-bender's power peters out, and as you shimmer into view, some in the mob glance your way. You're in no immediate danger, but fighting this many would be no mean feat. {[B] SOUND: Pounding of fists/clubs on gate.}

