You raise your voice in a rousing ballad, the words coming to you as easily as breath. You sing of a woman whose child was taken from her, and how she hunted, fought, and killed the kidnappers to bring her child home.

By the third chorus, the entire Grotto is singing with fists raised. Others begin painting, fantastic murals that glow and seem to come to life in the dim light.

Misha pulls instruments from her chiurgeon's bag. "These might serve as weapons," she says, "though I hate to see their use so perverted."

Even Lali demonstrates a few fighting moves her husband had taught her before he died, and Ashe spends the night running around slashing imaginary bad guys.

