Vostiel sighs. "Thought you had more spirit, captain, but fair enough. Command it is."

He falls in, and the company starts forward once more, with Landi trying not look smug, and the little Aeon Priest grumbling about unnecessary stops and a lack of concern for his safety.

{SOUND: More rain, more marching}

Though you can see that Vostiel is disappointed, he passes your orders on to his squads without question, just as Vandi does, and under their direction, your company retreats into the trenches to the right of the road with disciplined speed. They are so quick in fact, that the ambushers barely have to time to get off a few quick shots before your soldiers are all taking up defensive positions. Only two men are hit, and neither seriously. 

{SOUND: Energy weapon fire.}
{ART: Illustration - Diving for Cover}

Your squad slips between the patrols and spreads out behind a low ridge so the construct will have a harder time killing them all at once. They also mine the approaches to their positions in preparation for the inevitable counterattack. 

When all are in position, you give the signal, and the entire squad pours fire at the construct's front legs, where its synth plating is most damaged, hoping to collapse it. The construct's front legs buckle, and it topples.

Unfortunately, in the second that it takes it to fall on its face, it spits three beams in three different directions and half your squad is dead, burning like candles. {SOUND: Robot's Powerful Beam Weapon}

Pulling singularity detonators from your bandoliers, you and Vostiel slip through the enemy patrols and wait at the crest of a low ridge until the construct has gone behind the monolith, then you creep over. 

And that is how you proceed for the next half hour. Moving only when the monolith blocks the construct's motion sensors, then laying still as it limps around for another pass. 

Finally, you're within throwing range, and as the hulking mantis comes into view once more, you key your aether-com to signal the squad. At the click, they start firing from behind the ridge, drawing the eyes of both the construct and the troopers in the enclosure. Now's your chance!

{SOUND: Energy weapon fire.}
{ART: Illustration - Sniping the Construct}

"Why was I not retrieved in a vehicle?" he asks. "Why am I being escorted home by poorly armed grunts? If this is what Command thinks of my work, maybe I should sell it to the enemy!" {VOICE: The priest should be shrill, obviojusly. Extra sneer on "trench fodder."}

The woman marching beside you sighs. She has a lieutenant's pips on her tunic, and a scar on her right cheek from temple to jaw. "No vehicles available," she says. "And no other companies. Thanks to the Chalcedon's assault, every other company fit to fight is marching straight for Ansau."

With a curse, one of the men carrying the priest's package sinks to his waist in the mud. The column stops while the soldiers try to extricate him. Your lieutenant stands at attention. The priest watches with a smirk.

Avoiding the beam of the heater, you and your squad skulk swiftly through the trenches toward the dead construct. Unfortunately, the skirmishers have your position and change tacks to intercept, and before you get halfway there, two hulking quadrupedal shapes are bounding over the earthworks and leaping down at you. Frilled bauls!

You and Vostiel manage to cook one with your beamers before it lands on you, but the other downs one of your soldiers, mauling her with its claws and stinging her with its quills. 

{SOUND: Roaring lions.}
{ART: Illustration - Frilled bauls}

Your squad dives into an earth-roofed command post just as a pair of hulking frilled bauls leaps into the trench and charges in at you. You and Vostiel fire at them, burning white light through their hearts and brains, and they drop dead at your feet. 

The skirmishers pour in right behind them, however, firing in all directions before your squad can recover. Fortunately, they're shooting in the dark, and only one of your soldiers is hit. 

After that it's all hand to hand, and there you have an advantage. Your squad have fought in the Endless Battle for years, and are expert close-in fighters. It's over in less than a minute, and you have only lost two.

{SOUND: Roaring lions/dinosaurs.}
